Right, mostly by letting it. Treat each pattern like a hypothesis, not a home, then poke it with predictions and small bets. Pre-register in plain words what you expect, then check. Keep a disconfirm list, little notes on where it didn’t fit. Try to falsify your favorite strand. Ask, “what would change my mind?” And do that before the test. When a pattern survives contact, keep it provisional. When it doesn’t, learn and prune. And if it’s about relationships or self, consequences and feedback count more than elegant symmetry. It’s less “prove once” and more “keep checking.”